<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the TrollStore test build (DESIGN.md 37).

  These are EXACTLY the entitlements TrollStore grants an app it installs,
  minus get-task-allow. They were read off the device rather than guessed:
  TrollStore granted five, and this file reproduces four of them byte for byte.

  Why omit get-task-allow. It is iOS's debuggable flag, scored at +35 by
  ios_get_task_allow, and TrollStore sets it on everything it installs so its
  JIT option works. That gave every measurement from the test iPhone a
  permanent +35 floor, so the device could never read trusted and a clean iOS
  baseline was unobtainable. Suppressing it server-side with
  INTEGRITY_ALLOW_DEBUG was the wrong answer: that flag gates SIX rules across
  both platforms, including ios_process_traced (+50), so it would have blinded
  the server to a real debugger attaching.

  Why keep the other four unchanged, and keychain-access-groups in particular.
  The Secure Enclave key lives in the TROLLTROLL.* access group. Change that
  value and the existing key becomes unreachable, the app silently generates a
  new one, and the installation identity resets on every rebuild. Verified on
  device: after re-signing with this file, "Key created this launch" read No.

  Applied by tools/presign_trollstore_ipa.sh. TrollStore preserves entitlements
  present in the binary rather than overwriting them, which is what makes this
  work; that behaviour was confirmed on device, not assumed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>application-identifier</key>
	<string>TROLLTROLL.*</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>TROLLTROLL</string>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.container-required</key>
	<string>com.example.devicefingerprinting</string>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>TROLLTROLL.*</string>
		<string>com.apple.token</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
